  "body": {
    "id": "lem:positive-fraction-endpoint-exclusion",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every fixed member of \\(\\mathfrak M\\) and every admissible balanced pilot-size sequence \\(m=m(n)\\in\\mathcal M_n\\) such that \\(m/n\\to c\\) for some \\(c\\in(0,1)\\),\n\\[\n\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}n^{3/2}\\mathcal E_n(m)=\\infty.\n\\]\nThe lower bound is uniform over \\(\\beta\\in\\mathcal K\\) for the fixed member, but no common numerical bound over all members of \\(\\mathfrak M\\) is asserted.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "Model",
      "m",
      "Mbal",
      "N",
      "En",
      "beta",
      "Kclass"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:stable-product-law",
      "def:model-class",
      "def:exact-risk-components",
      "thm:oracle-deletion-loss",
      "prop:second-order-risk-reduction",
      "prop:first-order-arbitrary-action-optimality"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Fix a member of \\(\\mathfrak M\\) and let\n\\[\na_-=\\inf_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal K}a(\\beta)>0,\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nwhose positivity is part of \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}oracle\\text{-}deletion\\text{-}loss}\\).  Define\n\\[\nq_n(\\beta)=E_{S_m}\\inf_{H\\in\\mathcal H_T}\nN\\{V_T(H,\\beta)-V_T(G_T(\\beta),\\beta)\\},\n\\qquad T=T_{n,m}^{\\mathrm{main}}.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe first-order arbitrary-action proposition gives a deterministic sequence \\(\\epsilon_n\\downarrow0\\), depending on the fixed member, such that\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal K}q_n(\\beta)\\geq-\\epsilon_n.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nFor a pilot-measurable rule \\(\\delta_m\\), condition on \\(S_m\\) and the pilot information.  By the stable product law, the main-wave potential outcomes are conditionally independent of the pilot outcomes.  Hence the realized action \\(H=\\delta_m\\) is one of the actions over which the infimum in (21) is taken, and\n\\[\nnL_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta)\n\\geq \\frac Nn q_n(\\beta)\n\\geq-\\frac Nn\\epsilon_n.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nThe exact risk reduction \\(\\mathrm{prop{:}second\\text{-}order\\text{-}risk\\text{-}reduction}\\) gives\n\\[\nn\\{R_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta)-R_n^{\\mathrm{or}}(\\beta)\\}\n=nD_{n,m}(\\beta)+nL_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta).\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nSince \\(m/n\\to c\\in(0,1)\\), the macroscopic conclusion of \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}oracle\\text{-}deletion\\text{-}loss}\\) yields\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal K}\n|nD_{n,m}(\\beta)-c\\,a(\\beta)|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\nCombining (20)--(25), for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), every pilot rule and every \\(\\beta\\in\\mathcal K\\) satisfy\n\\[\nR_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta)-R_n^{\\mathrm{or}}(\\beta)\n\\geq \\frac{c a_-}{2n}>0.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nTaking the positive part, the supremum over \\(\\beta\\), and then the infimum over \\(\\delta_m\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_n(m)\\geq\\frac{c a_-}{2n}.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(n^{3/2}\\) proves the assertion.",
    "justification": "The positive deletion coefficient cannot be offset by any arbitrary main-wave action at first order.",
    "gap": "It does not address pilots smaller than a positive fraction.",
    "consumer": "It removes the fixed-positive-fraction endpoint from the unresolved global pilot question.",
